READ THE ROWS IN PAIRS AND THE ANSWER IS FLAT.
  . `eq` answers TRUE FOR EVERY MEMBER **with the star and without it**. The star
    is not what fixed anything. kant has no `eq` -- it is registered nowhere in
    incant/setup's Operators -- so the token falls through truthy instead of
    refusing. That is three-language drift: `eq` is tok's string compare, and
    `==` is tok's NUMERIC one, which is why the habit exists and why it is wrong
    here. In kant, `==` is opEQ and it compares values.
  . `btT == "return"` reading 0 is `:=` MINTING A HOLDER. `btT := taG` makes btT
    a holder whose body is a pointer, so a bare read of it is a read of the
    holder and never of the tag. `*btT` reads through. Bear-trap #41 is the
    parent -- "capture, then test" is advice about an OPERATOR -- and this is its
    other half: having captured with `:=`, READ IT BACK WITH `*`.
  . `*btCur.taG == "return"` reading 0 is the DOT defect, and it is the row that
    belongs to the `.`-is-binary ruling rather than to this file. A chained read
    through a cursor does not deliver the tag. Carry this row into that stroke's
    evidence list; it is a second witness beside the chained-subscript one.

